+*   **When not to use:** Scenes with purely diffuse materials where the added complexity of BRDF and illumination modeling provides no benefit [S1].
 
 ## Background
-3iGS was introduced to address the limitation of standard 3D Gaussian Splatting, which models outgoing radiance directly without considering the interdependencies of shared scene illumination [S1]. It differs from GaussianShader, which uses global differentiable environment lights stored in cube maps and independent Gaussians with spherical harmonic-based color [S1]. 3iGS represents incident illumination locally with grid-based tensors and optimizes Gaussian BRDF features relative to this field [S1]. The method is inspired by conventional computer graphics engines that use baking techniques for fast rendering [S1].
+3iGS (Factorised Tensorial Illumination for 3D Gaussian Splatting) was introduced by Z.J. Tang and T.J. Cham [S1]. It addresses the limitations of 3DGS in modeling specular effects by integrating concepts from neural radiance fields (specifically TensoRF) and computer graphics shading models into the Gaussian Splatting framework [S1]. It differs from GaussianShader [13] by using a local grid-based illumination field instead of global environment maps and optimizing BRDF features relative to this field [S1].
 
